WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.222
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.222 --> 00:00:05.540
Welcome to the MIT
Center for Art, Science,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.100
and Technology's 2021 symposium,
Unfolding Intelligence--

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.380
The Art and Science of
Contemporary Computation.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.930
CAST was established
in 2012, with the goal

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.480
of building and
building on connections

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.150
between the worlds of art,
science, and technology.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.220
This is the third in
a series of symposia

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.870
that CAST has
convened since then,

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:29.480
and as with its predecessors,
we bring together artists,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:33.340
scientists, engineers, and
humanists from within MIT

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.100
and from the world at
large to discuss areas

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.760
of rapidly evolving research
and urgent social relevance,

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.240
and to find in that dialogue
stimulation, confirmation,

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:49.390
provocation, intersection,
and, we hope, common purpose.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:52.690
At MIT, CAST partners with
departments, labs, and centers

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.120
to integrate the arts
across the curriculum

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.630
to enrich and encourage artistic
collaboration and to provide

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:01.750
support to faculty and
members of the MIT community

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.000
as they pursue their
own artistic practice

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.920
and/or research.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.980
In addition to
symposia like this,

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.530
CAST facilitates the sharing
of this creative work

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.050
beyond the Institute
by producing concerts,

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.660
exhibitions, and
publications, and making

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.310
them available to the public.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.980
So thank you for
being with us today.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.980
We hope you will join
us throughout the week

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.340
at virtual events addressing
the aesthetic, technical,

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.670
and critical issues pertaining
to artificial intelligence

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.010
and computational media.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:36.670
We also look forward to seeing
you on Friday, April 9th,

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:40.510
as the symposium culminates
with a live interactive event

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:44.200
to which all attendees are
invited, at which you can join

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.570
presenters and artists
in breakout rooms

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.850
to explore hidden
threads between all that

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:50.470
has been discussed this week.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:02.550
Welcome.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.410
My name is Caroline
Jones, and I'm

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:08.100
an art historian teaching in
the History Theory Criticism

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.130
section of the Department
of Architecture here at MIT.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:13.950
This is a pre-recorded
introduction

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.540
to the second of two sessions
on the theme of open systems

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.660
in the Unfolding
Intelligence Symposium hosted

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:26.160
by the Center for Art, Science,
and Technology, known as CAST.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.040
These sessions are also
supported by MIT'S Transmedia

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.320
Storytelling Initiative.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.990
Once again, I want to thank
[? Millin ?] postdoc Will

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.390
Lockett, and cast
director Leila Kinney

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.670
for making this
symposium a reality,

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.730
as well as Katherine Higgins,
who served as the Open Systems

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.690
session producer.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.180
Please feel free to ask
questions at any time

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:47.540
during the webcast.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.340
You'll also find links to
other events in the symposium.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.950
Our colleagues at the
List Visual Arts Center

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:57.670
have their own hosted related
events on artists and AI

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:01.710
that I hope you can
sign up for and attend.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.710
I want to begin my brief
introduction tonight

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:09.030
by speaking the words of
MIT's recently achieved land

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.710
acknowledgment.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:15.240
Although we gather virtually,
the resources and capacities

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.020
of MIT come from being
a land-grant university,

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.380
based on the ongoing
occupation and colonization

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.130
of previously occupied land.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.460
MIT acknowledges
Indigenous Peoples

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.100
as the traditional
stewards of this land,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.470
recognizing the
enduring relationship

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:38.430
that exists between them and
their traditional territories.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:42.150
The land on which we gather
is the traditional unceded

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.730
territory of the Wampanoag
Nation, among others.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.360
We acknowledge the painful
history of genocide

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.880
and the forced occupation
of their territory,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.360
and we honor and respect
the many diverse Indigenous

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.170
People connected to this
land from time immemorial.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.300
Acknowledging the living
indigenous communities here

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.330
in Massachusetts is
really important to me,

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:10.990
and this evening's session
opens directly onto questions

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.690
of indigenous
epistemologies of kinship

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:16.540
and biological
theories of mutualism

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.060
that our panelists will discuss.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.550
Following their
pre-recorded presentations,

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:23.470
we'll engage in
a live discussion

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:28.890
and Q&amp;A. Mutualism emerges
from Professor Fredrickson's

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:33.060
biological study of
entangled ecologies,

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.600
while professor Lewis
argues that kinship can also

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.020
be forged with our machine
learning prostheses,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.170
potentially as proxies for
other species with whom

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.680
we share the planet.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.240
The way that both of them
utilize computation and think

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:52.640
about the promise of AI is
the fascinating conversation

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.720
I look forward to engaging
them with tonight.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.510
We'll begin with Professor
Frederickson, followed

00:04:58.510 --> 00:04:59.500
by Professor Lewis.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.300
I'll then open
out our discussion

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:05.350
to your synchronous
questions and answers.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.570
Megan Frederickson
has been engaged

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.690
with the biology of
social insects, plants,

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:13.040
and their symbionts for decades.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:14.920
Educated at Harvard
as an undergrad,

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.860
she did her PhD at
Stanford and runs a lab

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.830
on mutualism,
ecology, and evolution

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.410
at the University of Toronto.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.870
Fredrickson's work on
mutualism and its role

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:29.500
in driving evolution requires
that biological events

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.420
be converted into data--

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.480
data that might be
necessary to convince

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.760
remnant Darwinists
obsessed with survival

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.790
of the fittest, predicated
on individual reproductive

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:42.970
success, that we actually need
to think about other units

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.700
of evolutionary fitness
and combinations

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:48.700
of communities that
evolve together.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.220
While field analysis
alone might inform us

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:56.220
about native and invasive ants
as seed spreaders, for example,

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.560
we need rapid throughput
genomic analysis

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.400
to tell us about
holobionts and hologenomes

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:06.030
in these complex
plant/animal societies.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.240
Megan is writing a book that
aims to synthesize mutualism

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.570
in microbiome research in
order to better understand when

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:15.870
and which microbes are
beneficial to their hosts,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:20.220
including humans, and how we can
make the most of microbes which

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.450
are everywhere on this planet.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.390
So for those at
Jenna Sutela's talk

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:27.960
this morning, you'll recognize
a consistent [? troubling ?]

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.500
of the cranial model of
intelligence, that idea

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.070
that a brain can be analogized
to a centralized computer

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:38.970
mainframe or motherboard
with its circuits and command

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:40.050
centers.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:43.410
Sutela and Frederickson
both point to the fact

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.320
that there's intelligence
in the microbial gut brain

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.420
that we are still
trying to figure out.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.210
There's also intelligence
in the immune brain that

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.730
learns and remembers from
its encounters with vaccines

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:54.930
and viruses.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:59.190
There is superorganismic
intelligence in the ant colony,

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.950
and even a kind of
homeostatic intelligence

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:05.140
in our beleaguered
planetary systems.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:08.110
Frederickson is building
a laboratory system

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:11.890
that wants to engage these
questions quite precisely.

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:15.280
She hopes to do massively
parallel host microbe

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.120
experiments in hopes of testing
the effects of thousands

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:23.620
of microbial communities, along
with their mutualist symbionts.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:25.120
This is important.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:27.370
When we assess the
impact of termites

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:29.710
on the African
ecology, for example,

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:33.370
we need to reckon with the fact
that these eusocial insects

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:38.170
don't even have the capacity
to digest wood in their guts

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:42.130
without symbiotic
protists carried along.

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:45.940
They form lifelong relationships
with quite different species

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:48.280
that must evolve
with them and are

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.950
responsible for
managing an ecosystem

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:52.630
dependent on their activities.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:58.130
By naming this
session Open System,

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:00.760
I aimed precisely to open
ideas of intelligence

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:03.110
to this kind of questioning--

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:07.130
the broader conversation about
models of sentience and ways

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:11.240
of knowing among and
between planetary forms.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.120
Frederickson uses genetic
analysis and thinks with

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:17.390
algorithms to trace evolutionary
changes among mutualists

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:20.900
in an ecosystem, such as
a recent paper about this

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.510
Caribbean plant known
as the yellow alder,

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:26.990
crucial to a particular
butterfly-- the tawny coster--

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:30.530
which lays its eggs on this
particular plant's leaves.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:32.960
The evolution of mating
and reproductive systems

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:36.620
involve all members of
this mutual relationship,

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:38.929
confronting the
tendency of biologists

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:42.409
to speak of parasites
and hosts or talk

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:45.440
about cheaters and altruists.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.900
Fredrickson's lab
offers a recent study

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:52.630
of rhizobial bacterial
genomics inside legume roots.

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:56.210
As she will discuss in her
pre-recorded presentation,

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.280
she and her colleagues
showed that the evolution

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:02.360
of bacterial bonds
solicited by these legumes

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.640
is what drives the
evolutionary outcome

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.500
of the mutualist system.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:08.810
Bacteria confer
fitness on the legumes

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:12.710
and evolved toward greater
mutualism, not less.

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:17.750
Bacteria are crucially part
of the environment, which also

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.030
is a partner in this dynamic.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:21.740
Although Frederickson
acknowledges

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:24.650
that the classical biological
definition of kinship

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:29.060
does not allow her to call these
symbiotic mutualisms of legumes

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:33.600
and their bacteria kinship,
maybe this symposium

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.110
will encourage her to
persuade biology otherwise.

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.210
Think how useful
such a concept could

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:44.190
be in describing a situation in
which a nitrogen-starved plant

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:47.310
sends out a chemical
signal for help, growing

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:51.180
nodules on its fruit to welcome
the necessary nitrogen-fixing

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:53.910
bacteria from the environment.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.000
In one recent analysis
by biochemist Maroti

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.180
and Kondorosi, exchanges
of signal molecules

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:03.240
between the partners,
bacteria and legume,

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:05.580
leads to the formation
of root nodules

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:11.340
where bacteria are converted
to nitrogen-fixing bacteroides.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.530
In other words,
the bacteria change

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:17.310
from free-dwelling in
the soil to endosymbionts

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.650
in their new dependency
on the legume

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:23.410
for photosynthesise nutrition.

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:26.620
Jason Edward Lewis
understands the power

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:29.710
of such non-sanguineous
kinships.

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:33.580
He brings into this discussion
an acute awareness of TEK--

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:37.090
traditional environmental
knowledge-- in confrontation

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:41.170
with our Western models
of technocratic mastery.

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:43.720
TEK produces an active
link with the teaching

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:47.110
of elders and ancestors,
which Lewis recently

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:50.410
celebrated in his native
Hawaii, and as it is recently

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:53.740
being re-evaluated in something
like the controlled burns

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:57.600
of the California high drylands.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:00.270
Those were originally
conducted by First Peoples,

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.700
dismissed by Western
management techniques,

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:07.510
and are now being re-solicited
to manage those fragile lands.

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:10.960
Like Frederickson, Lewis was
also educated at Stanford,

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.300
where he received a
degree in symbolic systems

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:16.270
before going to the Royal
College of Arts for an MPhil

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:18.340
degree in design.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:20.620
Professor Lewis is
both a creative coder

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:22.060
and a media artist.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:24.250
He has also been
a profound mentor

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:28.390
of generations of Indigenous
media theorists and artists.

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:34.570
Professor of Computation
Arts and University Research

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:37.360
Chair in Computational
Media, he also

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.820
runs the Initiative
for Indigenous Futures

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:43.360
from his post at
Concordia University.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:46.390
In all these endeavors,
Lewis has given us

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:50.590
an entirely new and fruitful
way of thinking about kin--

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:53.410
notably, in his
collaborative polemic,

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:57.070
published in MIT's own
Journal of Design and Science,

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:59.920
Making Kin With the Machines.

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:03.610
The authors, who identify
variously as Cree, Lakota,

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:06.370
and Hawaiian hold that
many different Indigenous

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:09.610
epistemologies
converge on methods

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:13.750
for making respectful
relationships with minerals,

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:18.670
microbes, and machines, as
well as animals, of course,

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:22.210
giving us forms of knowing
that can help us move away

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:26.560
from the anthropocentric
troubles we find ourselves in.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.650
As a scholar of Hawaiian,
Cherokee, and Samoan

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:32.650
descent himself, Lewis
puts these epistemologies

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:36.820
to work in his own creative
computational poetics.

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:40.960
I'm showing you here an excerpt
from his poem Quartet, which

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:43.630
falls on the 2019
science fiction book

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:46.900
by Adrian Tchaikovsky,
Children of Ruin,

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:51.670
about an octopoid intelligence
on a terraformed planet.

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:54.880
Lewis's poem segues
into an imagining

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:58.570
of a troika of AI
companions based

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:02.080
on the intelligence of
the honorable octopus put

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:05.530
at the service of a young
Hawaiian adolescent.

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:08.890
In addition to stimulating
Lewis's poetry,

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:13.960
this [? oco-akamai ?] AI is in
tune with deeply-held Hawaiian

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:17.650
concepts of responsibility
and reciprocity within

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:19.450
the ecosystem.

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:22.150
These poems and
meditations are published

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:28.000
within a document Lewis edited
in 2019 and published in 2020,

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.990
The Indigenous Protocol
for AI, an IP thinking

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:35.170
about the potentials
and colonizing perils

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:37.900
of artificial intelligence.

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:40.480
The document was the
pre-COVID achievement

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:45.700
of an extraordinary gathering
of scholars and thinkers,

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:49.150
including our own esteemed
colleague D. Fox Harrell,

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:52.330
who's running a different
session in this symposium.

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:55.090
These critical
approaches to IP and AI

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:58.270
want to open out those
systems to our scrutiny

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:01.420
as well as welcoming our
multigenerational and

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:04.640
multi-species imagination.

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:09.320
In conclusion, if this
morning's conversation

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:12.995
prompted artist Jenna Sutela
and curator-theorist Lars Bang

00:14:12.995 --> 00:14:17.450
Larsen to think about AI
as alien intelligence,

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:21.500
addressing how we might imagine
potential kinds of sentience

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.330
that our planet's
not-yet encountered,

00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:29.420
then this evening's prompts
us to think about AI as kin.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:32.060
Frederickson and
then Lewis will help

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:35.030
us unfold machine
intelligence as a tool

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:38.600
for revealing mutualisms and
a creative device for creating

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.180
more-than-human alliances.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:43.760
Let us turn, then, to
these amazing panelists

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:47.870
[? with ?] their open systems,
engaging life and machines.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:49.960
Thank you.

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:51.200
Hi, everyone.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:55.030
My name is Megan Frederickson,
and I'm a professor of Ecology

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:58.420
and Evolutionary Biology at
the University of Toronto.

00:14:58.420 --> 00:15:01.940
My talk today is entitled
Animal, Plant, and Microbial

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:05.940
Life, Interconnected.

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:08.960
So I study mutualism
and symbiosis,

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:12.650
two words that are often
used interchangeably,

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:15.560
but are not actually synonyms.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:18.950
So symbiosis means different
species living together

00:15:18.950 --> 00:15:22.070
in close association,
like corals

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:26.300
and their microscopic,
photosynthetic algae,

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:30.530
or leguminous plants and the
nitrogen-fixing bacteria they

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:34.340
house in root nodules, or
grazing mammals and their gut

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:36.540
microbes.

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:39.930
Mutualism, in contrast,
means all interactions

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:43.950
between species in which
both species or all species

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:46.440
benefit from the
interaction, whether or not

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:49.580
the species actually
live together.

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:55.550
Maybe most symbioses, or
probably most symbioses,

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:56.840
are mutualisms.

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:58.940
But not all mutualisms
are symbiotic.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.820
So for example, plants
and their pollinators

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:05.180
are generally mutually
beneficial interactions,

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:08.690
even though they don't
actually live together.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.880
And mutualisms in
symbiosis are everywhere,

00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:14.030
all around us all the time.

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:17.660
These interactions make
fundamental but often

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:22.070
underappreciated contributions
to ecosystem functions

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:24.380
that sustain biodiversity.

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:30.080
Globally, most pollination, seed
dispersal, biological nitrogen

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:33.470
fixation, photosynthesis
in coral reefs,

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:40.810
and digestion in mammalian
guts happens mutualistically.

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:44.530
Humans also have both
symbionts and mutualists.

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:48.820
We live in close association
with the diverse microorganisms

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:52.420
that colonize our bodies,
especially our guts.

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:54.910
Sometimes, this community
of microorganisms

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:57.550
is called the human
microbiome, which

00:16:57.550 --> 00:17:01.300
is currently a hot area
of research in biology.

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:04.240
And we can also think
about the interactions

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:09.220
that humans have with crops or
with livestock as mutualisms

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:12.010
in which both species benefit.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:16.270
So this is my son actually
picking strawberries at a farm

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:17.839
last summer.

00:17:17.839 --> 00:17:19.730
But I don't really study humans.

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:21.960
In fact, I don't even
study vertebrates.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:25.940
I study plants and
insects and microbes,

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.430
which are far more
diverse on our planet

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:30.680
than vertebrates are.

00:17:30.680 --> 00:17:33.500
And my research
aims to understand

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:38.870
how mutualisms and symbiosis
links the fate of interacting

00:17:38.870 --> 00:17:43.100
species in ecosystems, and
how these interconnections

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:48.180
between species have shaped
the evolution of life on Earth.

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:50.820
So mutualism is a
form of cooperation.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:54.630
And cooperation is an
extremely widespread phenomenon

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:58.440
at all levels of
biological organization.

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:00.570
We can think of
genes cooperating

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:04.740
in genomes, organelles
cooperating in cells,

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:07.620
cells cooperating in
multicellular bodies,

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:11.550
and individuals cooperating in
societies, including our own.

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:17.810
And mutualism is just
cooperation between species.

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:25.400
It's also so widespread
that most plants and animals

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:28.490
engage in mutualism with
at least one other species

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:32.060
that they depend on for
nutrition, or protection,

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:33.740
or dispersal.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:37.700
And I study mutualism and
symbiosis in the wild,

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:42.140
working at field stations
in both the Peruvian Amazon

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:45.180
and here in Canada
and elsewhere.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:48.740
And I also bring insects
and plants and microbes

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:52.910
into my lab at the University of
Toronto to study their traits,

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:55.520
or their behaviors,
or their genes

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:58.310
in ways that would be
impossible in the field.

00:18:58.310 --> 00:19:00.800
And increasingly, like
many other biologists,

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:04.880
I also use computational
and bioinformatics methods

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:09.160
to quantify patterns in
larger, complex data sets.

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:11.770
I'm showing you some
video of one interaction

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:15.520
that I've studied for many
years between an Amazonian plant

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:19.120
and the ants that
it feeds and houses.

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:22.622
So each branch on this
tree has a hollow swelling

00:19:22.622 --> 00:19:24.205
like the one you can
see in the middle

00:19:24.205 --> 00:19:26.870
of those leaves in the video.

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:30.310
And if you were to cut open that
hollow swelling on the branches

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:34.030
on this tree, you would
find them full of ants

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:36.580
and their eggs and larvae.

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:38.770
And this tree
species has actually

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:43.960
evolved to make this specialized
form of housing for ants.

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:46.730
And it also makes these
tiny little food bodies,

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:48.740
which are too small
to see in the video.

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:51.130
But it makes them to feed
the ant colonies that

00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:53.410
live inside its branches.

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:54.520
Why?

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:57.760
Well, these ants may be
small, but they are aggressive

00:19:57.760 --> 00:19:59.860
and have a really
powerful sting.

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:02.900
And they attack any herbivores
that land on the plant,

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:05.470
including this grasshopper
that you can see in the video--

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:08.020
or what's left of it anyways.

00:20:08.020 --> 00:20:10.960
So the ants defend the plant
against the plant's enemies,

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:13.930
and the plant nourishes
and shelters the ants.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:18.670
An excellent example of
cooperation between species.

00:20:18.670 --> 00:20:21.730
But cooperation is supposed
to have a dark side.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:24.400
Cooperation, in
all its forms, is

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:26.950
supposed to break down
because of the evolution

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:30.750
of selfish cheating strategies.

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:34.200
Evolutionary biologists
think about a population

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:35.940
of co-operators,
which are shown here

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.790
in the slide in the blue Cs.

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:41.340
And these co-operators--
these blue Cs--

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:43.950
benefit others in
their population

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:47.580
at some short-term
cost to themselves.

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:49.530
But they recoup
that short term cost

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:52.230
by also receiving benefits
from other co-operators

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:54.620
in their population.

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:56.960
But imagine if there
was a new genotype--

00:20:56.960 --> 00:20:58.610
the red D here--

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:02.210
that arises by mutation
in this population.

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:05.210
And what makes the
red genotype different

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:08.090
is that it no longer
provides any benefits

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:10.980
to the other members
of the population.

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:13.250
And thus, it saves on
the short-term costs

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:15.110
of cooperation.

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:18.290
But the red Ds still
receive benefits from others

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:21.410
in the population, and thus
have higher fitness than

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:24.020
the co-operators--
than the blue Cs--

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:26.510
because they get all
of the same benefits,

00:21:26.510 --> 00:21:29.270
but pay none of the costs.

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:34.040
Thus, the red D is a
cheater or defector genotype

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:36.530
that is favored by
natural selection.

00:21:36.530 --> 00:21:40.610
And we predict it should
spread in the population.

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:44.120
If all else is equal,
which it never is,

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:47.180
we would therefore expect
that the red D genotype

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:49.260
would spread to fixation.

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:52.760
And we would have started with
a population of co-operators--

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:54.020
the blue Cs--

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:59.480
and ended with a population of
non-co-operators-- the red Ds.

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:02.300
In other words, we
would expect cooperation

00:22:02.300 --> 00:22:05.310
to break down,
given enough time.

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:07.490
So why do we still
observe cooperation

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:11.520
in all its glorious
forms throughout nature?

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:15.990
How cooperation evolves
and is maintained in nature

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:18.990
despite the threat
of selfish cheating

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:21.840
is a question with
a very long history

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:23.820
in evolutionary biology.

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:26.820
And I'm not really going to
have time in my 15-minute talk

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:29.140
today to do it justice.

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:32.730
But very briefly, we have
known for a long time

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:36.360
that this puzzle is solved if
the donor and the recipient

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:39.675
of the cooperation are related,
meaning they share genes.

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:48.200
The British geneticist
J.B.S. Haldane

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:51.020
is said to have remarked
that he would not give up

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:53.600
his own life to
save one brother,

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:56.810
but he would lay down his
life for two brothers or eight

00:22:56.810 --> 00:22:57.860
cousins.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:22:58.520
Why?

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:01.770
Because in humans,
siblings share, on average,

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:03.890
half their genes,
and first cousins,

00:23:03.890 --> 00:23:06.710
share on average, an eighth.

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:10.100
The idea that
cooperation can evolve

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:13.100
if it's directed
at relatives or kin

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:16.040
was further formalized
by W.D. Hamilton

00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:19.730
in 1964 in a pair of
foundational papers.

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:22.400
And it's still one of
the leading explanations

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:25.190
for how cooperation
evolves between individuals

00:23:25.190 --> 00:23:26.780
of the same species.

00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:29.960
For example, in bees and
ants and other insects

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:34.150
that live in colonies,
as well as in humans.

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.580
But it doesn't work
for mutualisms.

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:39.310
Mutualisms, by definition,
occur between members

00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:42.610
of different species,
and are thus not kin.

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:45.770
Thus, Hamilton's explanation for
the evolution of cooperation,

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:47.800
which is often known
as kin selection,

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:50.140
cannot explain the
evolution of mutualism.

00:23:52.740 --> 00:23:56.070
Nonetheless, mutualism
and symbiosis clearly

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:57.413
evolve and persist in nature.

00:23:57.413 --> 00:23:58.830
They're all over
the place when we

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:00.990
look around the natural world.

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:04.995
And my research has repeatedly
shown that mutualism--

00:24:04.995 --> 00:24:07.620
oh, sorry-- that
cheating does not

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:09.750
threaten mutualism evolution.

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:12.600
I wrote an opinion article
a few years ago entitled

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:15.000
Mutualisms Are Not on
the Verge of Breakdown,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.760
arguing that despite a lot
of attention to the cheater

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:21.000
problem, we rarely actually
see cheaters in nature,

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:23.910
and there is little evidence
that mutualisms break down

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:26.640
into parasitisms over
evolutionary time.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:30.780
In short, mutualisms don't
go over to the dark side.

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:32.340
Moreover, there are
reasons to think

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:35.400
that the evolution of
mutualism and symbiosis

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:39.480
actually generates new life
forms and new functions that

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:43.120
can lead to ecological
and evolutionary success.

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:46.290
So to give just one
example, the eukaryotic cell

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:48.510
is the product of an
ancient symbiosis.

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:50.760
And of course, this
new kind of cell

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:54.060
gave rise to all the plants
and animals and fungi

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:56.430
that are on the planet today.

00:24:56.430 --> 00:24:59.280
As Lynn Margulis and
Dorion Sagan once wrote,

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:00.720
"life did not take
over the globe

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:03.420
by combat, but by networking."

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:06.690
So I want to briefly describe
one recent research project

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:09.510
from my lab to give you the
flavor of the kind of work

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:12.870
that I do, and also to
convince you that cooperation

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:15.730
can evolve easily and fast.

00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:19.830
So this project involves the
legume-rhizobia symbiosis shown

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:22.020
in cartoon form on this slide.

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:24.960
Leguminous plants-- including
many that you are familiar

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:26.430
with, like soybeans--

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:29.700
have symbiotic bacteria
called rhizobia that live

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:32.130
inside nodules on their roots.

00:25:32.130 --> 00:25:34.530
Those little
circles on the roots

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:37.800
are the nodules that house
these symbiotic bacteria.

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:41.070
And all plants need nitrogen.
And there's lots and lots

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:42.720
of nitrogen in the air.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:46.710
But it's in a form that
plants can't use for growth.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.290
But rhizobia and
some other bacteria

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:51.330
are capable of doing
something really special.

00:25:51.330 --> 00:25:54.420
They can take
nitrogen from the air

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:56.880
and turn it into
plant-accessible forms,

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:59.340
a process called
nitrogen fixation.

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:02.040
And of course, plants
can do something special

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:04.440
that a lot of bacteria can't do.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:06.210
And that is they
can photosynthesize.

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:08.550
And they can take carbon
dioxide from the air

00:26:08.550 --> 00:26:12.670
and turn it into sugar,
which bacteria also need.

00:26:12.670 --> 00:26:15.780
So legumes and rhizobia
form a symbiosis

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:18.300
in which they exchange
plant-fixed carbon

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:22.170
and bacteria-fixed nitrogen
to their mutual benefit.

00:26:22.170 --> 00:26:24.895
+ is economically and
ecologically important,

00:26:24.895 --> 00:26:26.520
because there are
many crops and forage

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:28.560
plants that are
symbiotic legumes,

00:26:28.560 --> 00:26:32.580
and because biological nitrogen
fixation done by bacteria

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:37.120
is really critical to nutrient
cycling in ecosystems.

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:42.870
So in my lab, we've done a lot
of work on how in the wild,

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:47.580
there's lots of variation among
rhizobia in how much nitrogen

00:26:47.580 --> 00:26:51.000
they can fix and how much
they help their plants grow.

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.550
And we wanted to find out
whether we could evolve

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:55.750
better symbionts in the lab.

00:26:55.750 --> 00:27:00.120
So we took rhizobia and
added them to sterile plants,

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:01.440
and allowed them to grow.

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:03.270
And when the plants
got big enough,

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:05.220
we cut off the
above-ground plant parts--

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:06.810
so the leaves and the stems--

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:09.390
and let the roots die,
and re-released bacteria

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:10.920
into the soil.

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:14.040
Then, we planted another seed
of the same type of plant,

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:16.050
and repeated this
process five times

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:19.170
in a greenhouse experiment
lasting about a year.

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:21.840
We used five different
plant genotypes--

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:24.390
you can think of genotypes
as different varieties

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:26.460
of the same species of plant--

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:30.030
always giving the bacteria a
new seed of the same plant type.

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:32.140
And at the end of
the experiment,

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:34.590
we isolated and
cultured the microbes,

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:36.360
tested them on new
host plants, and then

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:38.760
measured how many
nodules they formed

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:41.710
and how much they
helped plants to grow.

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:44.670
So this is what the experiment
looked like in the greenhouse,

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:47.750
with bacteria evolving
in those pots of soil

00:27:47.750 --> 00:27:49.125
beneath all those
little legumes.

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:53.600
And this is what we found.

00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:55.300
So each one of
these panels labeled

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:58.300
with a number across the top
is a different plant genotype

00:27:58.300 --> 00:27:59.470
or variety.

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:02.140
And we tested the bacteria
at the end of the experiment

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:05.470
both on the plant genotype
that they evolved on

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:08.117
in the greenhouse experiment
and on all the other kinds

00:28:08.117 --> 00:28:09.700
of plant phenotypes,
which they hadn't

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:13.440
seen during their yearlong
evolution in the greenhouse.

00:28:13.440 --> 00:28:15.480
And that's indicated
on the x-axis

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:18.750
as whether the bacteria
shared evolutionary history

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:20.410
with their host.

00:28:20.410 --> 00:28:24.690
So the left-hand panel shows how
much the bacteria helped plants

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:26.460
to grow, how large
the plants grew

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:28.470
with different
kinds of bacteria,

00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:31.320
and the right-hand panel shows
how many nodules those bacteria

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:33.280
formed on plants.

00:28:33.280 --> 00:28:37.080
And what I hope you can see
is that in general, bacteria

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:39.390
formed more nodules
and made plants

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:42.990
grow bigger when they had
evolved with that plant

00:28:42.990 --> 00:28:44.760
genotype for a year.

00:28:44.760 --> 00:28:47.970
So the microbes got better
at cooperating with plants.

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:50.430
They became better mutualists,
but only with the host

00:28:50.430 --> 00:28:55.240
they were paired with during the
year-long evolution experiment.

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:58.470
So I've just shown you that
we can evolve better symbionts

00:28:58.470 --> 00:28:59.520
in the lab.

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:01.530
We can make microbes
that are more

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:03.450
cooperative with their hosts.

00:29:03.450 --> 00:29:05.670
And one current big
idea in the field

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:09.330
is that it might be possible
to evolve or engineer

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:13.830
microbial symbionts for a wide
range of beneficial purposes,

00:29:13.830 --> 00:29:17.490
be those applications in
health or in agriculture

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:20.820
or in biodiversity conservation.

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:24.060
Maybe we can design microbial
strains or communities

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:28.910
that improve human or
ecosystem health or well-being.

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:31.500
But the main problem is that
microbes are not, in fact,

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:32.490
machines.

00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:34.710
They are living organisms
that do not just

00:29:34.710 --> 00:29:36.070
stay where you put them.

00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:39.000
They do not necessarily
work in predictable ways,

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:41.220
or as the designer intended.

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:44.070
They disperse, they jump
from one house to another,

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:45.510
and they evolve.

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:47.130
So I think the jury
is still out on

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:50.850
whether evolving or engineering
microbiomes will really

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:54.318
work as some have envisioned.

00:29:54.318 --> 00:29:56.610
So what does this all mean
for artificial intelligence?

00:29:56.610 --> 00:30:01.740
I haven't really said much about
AI today, and I am no expert,

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:02.730
but I will say this.

00:30:02.730 --> 00:30:05.520
In my area of biology,
we often think

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:10.320
about microbes behaving
according to simple algorithms,

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:15.360
and evolving, and interacting
in tiny experimental microcosms

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:19.440
as just one step removed from
a simulation model running

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:21.000
on the computer.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:25.320
Microbes in the lab add
only a little bit of realism

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:28.080
above what happens in
silico, and they are still

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:32.340
a far cry from the complexity
of real natural ecosystems.

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:34.020
So in my lab, we
spend a lot of time

00:30:34.020 --> 00:30:37.560
thinking about what
conditions and designs select

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:41.850
for cooperation among beings
that don't share genes,

00:30:41.850 --> 00:30:44.490
and when, if ever,
we would expect

00:30:44.490 --> 00:30:48.030
to see the evolution of
selfishness or cheating.

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:50.640
And this seems like also
a worthwhile question

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:53.940
to ponder as humans
design and create

00:30:53.940 --> 00:30:56.460
ever more intelligent machines.

00:30:56.460 --> 00:30:58.650
And with that, I'd
like to acknowledge

00:30:58.650 --> 00:31:01.740
the people, institutions, and
funders that have supported

00:31:01.740 --> 00:31:02.760
my recent research.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:06.000
And I look forward
to the discussion.

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:06.880
Hello, everybody.

00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:09.540
I want to start by thanking
the organizers for inviting me

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:13.410
to this panel, and to Dr. Jones,
in particular, for moderating

00:31:13.410 --> 00:31:15.180
and bringing Megan
and I together

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:17.370
for preliminary conversation.

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:20.310
I am speaking from Tiohtiake,
known in settler Canada

00:31:20.310 --> 00:31:23.880
as Montreal, on the traditional
territory of the Kanienkehaka

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:25.560
Nation.

00:31:25.560 --> 00:31:27.540
I want to preface
these brief comments

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:30.360
by noting that as I use
the term Indigenous,

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:32.790
I'm talking specifically
of a North American Pacific

00:31:32.790 --> 00:31:36.332
Islander and Australia
and New Zealand context.

00:31:36.332 --> 00:31:38.040
And I want to underline
that I'm not here

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:40.617
to speak on behalf of
any Indigenous peoples,

00:31:40.617 --> 00:31:43.200
but rather to report on the work
that my Indigenous colleagues

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:45.652
and I have been doing.

00:31:45.652 --> 00:31:48.110
So today, I'm going to talk
about the work of an Indigenous

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:51.110
protocol and artificial
intelligence working group.

00:31:51.110 --> 00:31:53.180
For me, that story
starts with Making

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:55.580
Kin with the Machines,
an article I co-wrote

00:31:55.580 --> 00:32:00.200
in 2018 with three colleagues:
Noelani Arista, a historian who

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:03.060
was based at the University
of Hawaii at the time;

00:32:03.060 --> 00:32:05.450
Archer Pechawis, a Cree
performance artist who's

00:32:05.450 --> 00:32:10.070
based in Toronto; and
Suzanne Kite, a Lakota PhD

00:32:10.070 --> 00:32:11.390
candidate who works with me.

00:32:15.530 --> 00:32:17.600
Our goal was to
try to understand

00:32:17.600 --> 00:32:19.280
what artificial
intelligence might

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:22.990
mean within our particular
Indigenous context.

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:25.630
In the essay, we write,
"We believe that Indigenous

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:28.960
epistemologies are much better
and respectfully accommodating

00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:30.710
the non-human.

00:32:30.710 --> 00:32:32.330
We retain a sense
of community that

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:34.880
is articulated through
complex kin networks anchored

00:32:34.880 --> 00:32:38.690
in specific territories,
genealogies, and protocols.

00:32:38.690 --> 00:32:41.270
Ultimately, our goal is
that we, as a species,

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:43.580
figure out how to treat
these new, non-human kin

00:32:43.580 --> 00:32:45.703
respectfully and reciprocally."

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:51.900
One way to think about this
is to understand that each

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:52.680
of our cultures--

00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:54.780
Hawaiian, Kree, and Lakota--

00:32:54.780 --> 00:32:56.730
when confronted with a
new entity, ask first,

00:32:56.730 --> 00:32:58.410
how are you related to me?

00:32:58.410 --> 00:33:00.840
Rather than, what is that?

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:03.720
That is true for other humans,
certainly, but also animals,

00:33:03.720 --> 00:33:06.067
stones, mountains, and oceans.

00:33:06.067 --> 00:33:07.650
That first question
illustrates a view

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:09.192
of the world that
sees all encounters

00:33:09.192 --> 00:33:12.420
through a relational lens
first, even before essence,

00:33:12.420 --> 00:33:16.690
and certainly before utility.

00:33:16.690 --> 00:33:18.280
How each of our
cultures articulates

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:20.497
that relationality is
expressed through protocol--

00:33:20.497 --> 00:33:23.080
in other words, the procedures
for recognizing and maintaining

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:24.610
those relationships.

00:33:24.610 --> 00:33:27.700
Protocol is very important
within our cultures,

00:33:27.700 --> 00:33:30.205
as it guides us in how
to live a good life.

00:33:30.205 --> 00:33:32.080
For instance, Hawaiians
understand themselves

00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:34.960
as links in a genealogical
chain that places us

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:37.180
in a kinship relationship
with the ocean

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:38.950
and the creatures
of sea and land.

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:42.130
As Dr. Arista writes,
"Kanaka maoli ontologies

00:33:42.130 --> 00:33:44.170
make it difficult and
outright unrewarding

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:47.770
to reduce pono, or balance,
to a measure of one,

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:51.760
to prioritize the benefit of
individuals over relationships.

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:54.070
Healthy and fruitful balance
requires multiplicity

00:33:54.070 --> 00:33:57.820
and a willingness to continually
think in and through relation,

00:33:57.820 --> 00:34:00.550
even when-- perhaps
particularly when--

00:34:00.550 --> 00:34:03.550
engaging with those
different from ourselves.

00:34:03.550 --> 00:34:05.890
A Kanaka maoli approach
to understanding AI

00:34:05.890 --> 00:34:09.280
might seek to attend to the
power of mana that is exchanged

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:11.860
and shared between humans.

00:34:11.860 --> 00:34:13.719
In attending to
questions of mana,

00:34:13.719 --> 00:34:15.820
I emphasize our
preference of reciprocity

00:34:15.820 --> 00:34:18.489
and relationship-building
to take the pono--

00:34:18.489 --> 00:34:20.800
here meaning good or benefit--

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:24.267
take the pono of those in
relation into consideration."

00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:33.550
Cree people situate everything
in a circle of relationships.

00:34:33.550 --> 00:34:37.000
As Mr Pechawis writes,
"In Cree epistemology,

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:39.280
relationship is paramount.

00:34:39.280 --> 00:34:41.500
Nehiyawewin, or
Plains Cree Language,

00:34:41.500 --> 00:34:43.840
divides everything into
two primary categories:

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:45.460
animate and inanimate.

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:47.350
One is not better
than the other;

00:34:47.350 --> 00:34:49.620
they are merely different
states of being.

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:51.159
These categories are flexible.

00:34:51.159 --> 00:34:53.199
Certain toys are
inanimate until a child

00:34:53.199 --> 00:34:56.170
is playing with them, during
which time they are animate.

00:34:56.170 --> 00:34:57.970
A record player is
considered animate,

00:34:57.970 --> 00:35:02.020
while a record, radio, or
television set is inanimate.

00:35:02.020 --> 00:35:04.570
But animate or
inanimate, all things

00:35:04.570 --> 00:35:07.780
have a place in our circle
of kinship, or wahkohtowin."

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:14.858
Stones exemplify the
complex set of protocols

00:35:14.858 --> 00:35:16.650
that govern how the
Lakota approach kinship

00:35:16.650 --> 00:35:18.090
with the non-human.

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:21.600
Ms. Kite write, "Standing
Cloud, or Bill Stover,

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:23.610
communicates Lakota
ethics and ontology

00:35:23.610 --> 00:35:27.090
through speaking about
the interiority of stones.

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:28.770
These ancestors that
I have in my hand

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:31.050
are going to speak through me,
so that you will understand

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:32.310
the things that
they see happening

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:33.893
in this world and
the things that they

00:35:33.893 --> 00:35:36.390
know to help all people.

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:38.100
Stones are considered ancestors.

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:39.810
Stones actively speak.

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:41.820
Stones speak through
and to humans.

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:44.160
Stones see and know.

00:35:44.160 --> 00:35:46.350
Most importantly,
stones want to help.

00:35:46.350 --> 00:35:49.590
The agency of stones connects
directly to the question of AI,

00:35:49.590 --> 00:35:52.590
as AI is formed not only
from code, but from materials

00:35:52.590 --> 00:35:53.940
of the Earth.

00:35:53.940 --> 00:35:56.340
To remove the concept of
AI from its materiality

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:58.290
is to sever this connection.

00:35:58.290 --> 00:36:01.410
In forming a relationship to
AI, we form a relationship

00:36:01.410 --> 00:36:02.865
to the minds and the stones."

00:36:07.860 --> 00:36:10.680
If all things might
potentially be a relation,

00:36:10.680 --> 00:36:12.930
this certainly includes
the tools we make.

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:14.490
In the field of
AI, we are crafting

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:17.880
systems whose goal is to be
human-like in intelligence,

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:20.880
to which we are giving agency
we've previously only given

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:24.450
to humans, which are being
used to assess human behavior--

00:36:24.450 --> 00:36:27.360
and in some cases, make
judgments about that behavior--

00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:30.930
and which, on the outside edge
of the science fiction dream,

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:33.810
may achieve some form
of sentience, maybe even

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:35.340
consciousness.

00:36:35.340 --> 00:36:38.580
These are systems in which
we already are in relation,

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:40.050
and that relationship
is only going

00:36:40.050 --> 00:36:43.590
to grow and become more complex
as the technology advances.

00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:46.650
Indigenous cultures have
tools, languages, protocols,

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:50.010
and epistemologies that
remain robust in articulating

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:51.880
these types of relationships.

00:36:54.820 --> 00:36:56.950
As the Making Kin with the
Machines essay was being

00:36:56.950 --> 00:36:59.050
written, I worked with
two other colleagues--

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:01.510
[? Angie ?] [? Abdullah, ?]
a Trawlwoolway designer based

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:03.850
in Sydney; and
Oiwi Parker-Jones,

00:37:03.850 --> 00:37:08.130
a Hawaiian neuroscientist
working at Oxford.

00:37:08.130 --> 00:37:12.572
We put together two workshops in
the winter and spring of 2019.

00:37:12.572 --> 00:37:15.030
These were called the Indigenous
Protocol and AI Workshops,

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:17.490
and they were attended
by 35 participants

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:19.860
with representation from
15 indigenous communities

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:24.120
from across Aotearoa, Australia,
North America, and the Pacific.

00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:26.850
One of the Unfolding
Intelligences organizers, Dr.

00:37:26.850 --> 00:37:29.550
Fox Harrell, and his graduate
student, Danielle Olson,

00:37:29.550 --> 00:37:33.470
were among those who joined
us to lend their good minds.

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:35.060
The group included
neuroscientists,

00:37:35.060 --> 00:37:37.670
cultural knowledge holders,
computer scientists, poets,

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:40.430
language keepers, visual
artists, hula teachers,

00:37:40.430 --> 00:37:42.980
venture capitalists,
historians, software engineers,

00:37:42.980 --> 00:37:45.380
language keepers, and others.

00:37:45.380 --> 00:37:47.610
The workshop conversation
covered extensive ground,

00:37:47.610 --> 00:37:50.990
including epistemology, cultural
protocols, machine learning,

00:37:50.990 --> 00:37:54.350
colonization, temporal
models, ontology, sentience

00:37:54.350 --> 00:37:57.890
versus consciousness, software
architectures, and linguistics.

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:03.790
Out of those conversations came
the Indigenous Protocol and AI

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:07.690
Position Paper, which we
published in July of 2020.

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:09.190
Indigenous
epistemologies generally

00:38:09.190 --> 00:38:11.470
approach the discovery,
refinement, transmission,

00:38:11.470 --> 00:38:15.310
and preservation of knowledge
as inherently multidisciplinary.

00:38:15.310 --> 00:38:18.130
As such, the Position Paper
features artistic excursions

00:38:18.130 --> 00:38:21.130
as well as analytical
essays, and poetry as well as

00:38:21.130 --> 00:38:24.160
profiles of prototypes
built during the workshops.

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:26.440
Here's a small sampling.

00:38:26.440 --> 00:38:28.720
Suzanne Kite,
mentioned previously,

00:38:28.720 --> 00:38:31.420
wrote a chapter called How
to Build Anything Ethically.

00:38:31.420 --> 00:38:33.640
She examines the protocol
her people, the Lakota,

00:38:33.640 --> 00:38:35.590
use to build sweat
lodges in order

00:38:35.590 --> 00:38:38.350
to develop a methodology for
building computational systems

00:38:38.350 --> 00:38:39.190
in a good way.

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:44.050
Michelle Brown is
another PhD student

00:38:44.050 --> 00:38:47.740
who works for us from the
University of Hawaii at Manoa.

00:38:47.740 --> 00:38:49.450
She wrote a chapter
on how she imagines

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:52.240
for Indigenous [? Basque ?]
community harness harnessing AI

00:38:52.240 --> 00:38:56.580
to support them in their
relationships with the ocean.

00:38:56.580 --> 00:38:58.650
Given the centrality of
the eel to her culture,

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:01.410
she designed the technology
to mimic eel behavior,

00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:06.300
creating the Txitxardin,
an oceangoing AI relative.

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:08.130
One of my contributions
is to imagine

00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:10.740
a young Hawaiian growing up
alongside three different AI

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:11.890
systems.

00:39:11.890 --> 00:39:14.160
One is modeled after
process-oriented Indigenous

00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:18.270
languages, such as Blackfoot, as
described by Leroy Little Bear.

00:39:18.270 --> 00:39:20.700
One takes its foundation
in Hawaiian epistemologies

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:21.810
of abundance.

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:24.390
And the third uses the neural
architecture of an octopus

00:39:24.390 --> 00:39:27.000
to translate between the first
two and advise the youth.

00:39:30.258 --> 00:39:31.800
We also had a project
that prototyped

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:35.520
a piece of Indigenous design
language translation software.

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:38.110
Noe Arista, Caroline Running
Wolf, Michael Running Wolf,

00:39:38.110 --> 00:39:41.040
Caleb Moses, and Joel
Davison worked together

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:44.247
to create Hia Ki'i, an app that
allows you to use your camera

00:39:44.247 --> 00:39:46.080
phone to look at objects
in your environment

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:48.015
and get community-verified
Hawaiian language

00:39:48.015 --> 00:39:48.890
descriptions of them.

00:39:51.960 --> 00:39:54.120
And Dr. Hemi Whaanga
wrote about the potential

00:39:54.120 --> 00:39:56.220
for AI systems and
related technologies

00:39:56.220 --> 00:39:58.260
to be used against
Indigenous peoples

00:39:58.260 --> 00:40:01.920
as an extension of colonial
practices of exploitation,

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:04.693
extraction, and
control, particularly

00:40:04.693 --> 00:40:07.110
those that displace a people's
understanding of themselves

00:40:07.110 --> 00:40:09.900
with a worldview that
favors the colonizer.

00:40:09.900 --> 00:40:13.350
He writes, "The colonization of
the culture, language, and mind

00:40:13.350 --> 00:40:15.720
takes place through the
transmission of mental habits

00:40:15.720 --> 00:40:17.880
and contents by means
of social systems

00:40:17.880 --> 00:40:20.530
other than the
colonial structure.

00:40:20.530 --> 00:40:22.630
These social systems
include cultural practices,

00:40:22.630 --> 00:40:26.187
language, and technologies, such
as artificial intelligence."

00:40:28.713 --> 00:40:31.380
We also developed guidelines for
Indigenous-centered AI designed

00:40:31.380 --> 00:40:34.320
to reflect in condensed form
the themes and concerns that we

00:40:34.320 --> 00:40:35.997
found most important.

00:40:35.997 --> 00:40:37.830
I want to highlight
several of those themes.

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:42.920
Relationality and
its consequence,

00:40:42.920 --> 00:40:45.290
reciprocity, are the
core of humanness,

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:48.050
and are how we produce
knowledge that is worth knowing.

00:40:48.050 --> 00:40:50.030
AI systems should be
designed to understand

00:40:50.030 --> 00:40:52.940
how humans and
non-humans are related to

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:55.610
and interdependent
on each other.

00:40:55.610 --> 00:40:57.680
Understanding,
supporting, formalizing,

00:40:57.680 --> 00:41:00.310
and encoding these relationships
is a primary design goal.

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:10.560
Indigenous knowledge is often
rooted in specific territories.

00:41:10.560 --> 00:41:13.020
AI systems should be
designed hyperlocally,

00:41:13.020 --> 00:41:15.120
meaning in partnership
with specific Indigenous

00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:17.130
communities, and
should be responsible

00:41:17.130 --> 00:41:19.530
to those communities,
provide relevant support,

00:41:19.530 --> 00:41:23.530
and be accountable to those
communities first and foremost.

00:41:23.530 --> 00:41:25.180
And finally, all
AI systems should

00:41:25.180 --> 00:41:27.910
be designed to respect and
support data sovereignty.

00:41:27.910 --> 00:41:29.380
Indigenous communities
must control

00:41:29.380 --> 00:41:31.700
how their data is solicited,
collected, analyzed,

00:41:31.700 --> 00:41:33.370
and operationalized.

00:41:33.370 --> 00:41:36.340
They decide when to protect
it and when to share it,

00:41:36.340 --> 00:41:39.310
where the cultural intellectual
property rights reside

00:41:39.310 --> 00:41:42.910
or if they even want to deal
with that sort of framework,

00:41:42.910 --> 00:41:44.480
and to whom those rights adhere.

00:41:47.180 --> 00:41:49.910
So I want to close with
two provocations that

00:41:49.910 --> 00:41:50.900
grow out of this work.

00:41:54.450 --> 00:41:57.390
The AI industrial complex
doesn't have an ethics problem.

00:41:57.390 --> 00:41:59.860
It has an epistemology problem.

00:41:59.860 --> 00:42:01.630
The persistent
infections of bias

00:42:01.630 --> 00:42:04.860
are simply symptoms
of this deeper issue.

00:42:04.860 --> 00:42:07.377
The epistemology problem stems
from a series of assumptions

00:42:07.377 --> 00:42:09.210
that are built into how
we design and deploy

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:11.760
our computational
systems: the users

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:13.980
is an individual, the
individual is primarily

00:42:13.980 --> 00:42:17.220
concerned with maximizing
their personal well-being,

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:19.440
culture is a second-order
effect, rather than

00:42:19.440 --> 00:42:22.680
the primary phenomenon, and that
the only useful knowledge is

00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:27.420
that produced through
rational instrumentality.

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:30.390
This makes AI system engineers
blind to important aspects

00:42:30.390 --> 00:42:34.290
of human existence, such as
trust, care, and community,

00:42:34.290 --> 00:42:36.810
that are fundamental to how
human intelligence actually

00:42:36.810 --> 00:42:38.250
operates.

00:42:38.250 --> 00:42:40.590
The refusal to engage,
explore, and operationalize

00:42:40.590 --> 00:42:43.230
knowledge frameworks that
centralize these aspects

00:42:43.230 --> 00:42:46.410
is a tremendous
scientific failing,

00:42:46.410 --> 00:42:48.420
creating huge gaps
between what humans think

00:42:48.420 --> 00:42:50.400
of as an intelligent
presence in the world

00:42:50.400 --> 00:42:54.260
and what the industry
academic complex is building.

00:42:54.260 --> 00:42:57.300
This, to me, is the cause of
the white supremacy that social

00:42:57.300 --> 00:42:59.870
and computer scientists such
as [? Nobel, ?] [? Benjamin, ?]

00:42:59.870 --> 00:43:03.620
[? Gabru, ?] and
[? Bulameni ?] have documented.

00:43:03.620 --> 00:43:06.230
The bias in these systems
is not a bug, but rather

00:43:06.230 --> 00:43:08.930
a feature of an interlocking set
of knowledge systems designed

00:43:08.930 --> 00:43:11.630
over centuries to
privilege white men.

00:43:11.630 --> 00:43:13.400
We need to be clear about this.

00:43:16.830 --> 00:43:19.060
Furthermore, I have to
wonder if machine learning,

00:43:19.060 --> 00:43:21.352
deep learning, and all the
different AI techniques that

00:43:21.352 --> 00:43:23.280
rely on vast amounts
of data to work

00:43:23.280 --> 00:43:26.730
are not fundamentally
ethically compromised.

00:43:26.730 --> 00:43:28.350
Very little of that
data was collected

00:43:28.350 --> 00:43:29.795
in a truly ethical manner.

00:43:29.795 --> 00:43:31.170
The people who
produced that data

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:33.420
were not asked if
it be used this way.

00:43:33.420 --> 00:43:35.670
They were not
compensated for this use,

00:43:35.670 --> 00:43:39.880
and the use most likely does
not benefit them directly.

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:42.940
Indigenous people have a long
history with people like this--

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:46.480
scientists who behave as if our
data is a publicly available

00:43:46.480 --> 00:43:48.400
resource that they can
use to make themselves

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:51.850
famous or wealthy and
powerful, all in the name

00:43:51.850 --> 00:43:54.670
of the greater good, of
increasing humanity's store

00:43:54.670 --> 00:43:55.810
of knowledge.

00:43:55.810 --> 00:43:59.050
Indigenous folks see people
like that coming a mile away,

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:01.870
and recognize them for
what they are: colonizers.

00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:08.305
AI system builders who do
not understand the cultures

00:44:08.305 --> 00:44:10.680
in which they operate cannot
understand how their systems

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:12.150
actually perform.

00:44:12.150 --> 00:44:14.010
As scientists, they
should be ashamed at how

00:44:14.010 --> 00:44:15.570
narrow-minded they are.

00:44:15.570 --> 00:44:18.630
As engineers, they should
be embarrassed at how badly

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:21.270
their technology
fits their use case.

00:44:21.270 --> 00:44:22.670
We should expect more from them.

00:44:25.350 --> 00:44:26.940
In the meanwhile,
my colleagues and I

00:44:26.940 --> 00:44:29.190
will continue building
capacity within our communities

00:44:29.190 --> 00:44:31.110
so that we can design
and build AI systems

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:33.540
ourselves that are
better suited to helping

00:44:33.540 --> 00:44:35.670
our particular
communities thrive--

00:44:35.670 --> 00:44:37.500
ones that look beyond
impoverished models

00:44:37.500 --> 00:44:41.502
of intelligence to develop ones
that are based on abundance.

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:45.040
Thank you.

00:44:45.040 --> 00:44:48.170
I am looking forward
to the conversation.

